A module of optional values, result values, panic helpers, and byte utilities.

A public record Option of T has a has value (a truth value), and a value (T).

A public record Result of T and E has an is ok (a truth value), an ok val (T), and an err val (E).

Publicly, to wrap option for T given value as T, named option_some, giving Option of T.
Let value out be Option of T.
Set value out's has value to true.
Set value out's value to value.
Give back value out.

Publicly, to empty option for T, named option_none, giving Option of T.
Let value out be Option of T.
Give back value out.

Publicly, to inspect option for T given candidate as a pointer to Option of T, named option_is_some, giving a truth value.
Give back candidate's has value.

Publicly, to unwrap option for T given candidate as a pointer to Option of T, fallback as T, named option_unwrap_or, giving T.
Give back candidate's value if candidate's has value equals true, otherwise give back fallback.

Publicly, to succeed result for T and E given value as T, named result_ok, giving Result of T and E.
Let value out be Result of T and E.
Set value out's is ok to true.
Set value out's ok val to value.
Give back value out.

Publicly, to fail result for T and E given failure as E, named result_err, giving Result of T and E.
Let value out be Result of T and E.
Set value out's is ok to false.
Set value out's err val to failure.
Give back value out.

Publicly, to inspect result for T and E given candidate as a pointer to Result of T and E, named result_is_ok, giving a truth value.
Give back candidate's is ok.

Publicly, to unwrap result for T and E given candidate as a pointer to Result of T and E, fallback as T, named result_unwrap_or, giving T.
Give back candidate's ok val if candidate's is ok equals true, otherwise give back fallback.

Publicly, to halt msg (a pointer to byte) with length len (an unsigned number), named panic.
Ask Linux kernel 1 with 2, msg, and len.
Ask Linux kernel 1 with 2, "\n", and 1.
Ask Linux kernel 60 with 1.

Publicly, to check stop given cond as a truth value, msg as a pointer to byte, len as an unsigned number, named assert_true.
If not cond, halt msg with length len.

Publicly, to zero bytes at dst (a pointer to byte) for len (an unsigned number), named mem_zero.
Let i be 0 as an unsigned number.
While i is less than len, set item i of dst to 0, add 1 to i.

Publicly, to compare bytes at a (a pointer to byte) with bytes at b (a pointer to byte) for len (an unsigned number), named mem_eq, giving a truth value.
Let i be 0 as an unsigned number.
While i is less than len, set i to i plus 1, if a[i minus 1] is not equal to b[i minus 1], give back false.
Give back true.
